$\begin{align}
y^6\cdot y^3&=y^{6+3}
\\\\
&=y^{9}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $x^m\cdot x^n=x^{m+n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
y^6\cdot y^3&=\underbrace{y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y}_\text{6 times}\cdot\underbrace{y\cdot y\cdot y}_\text{3 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y}_\text{9 times}
\\\\
&=y^{9}
\end{align}$
